  {
    "episode_id": 64553,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/4/64553.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "Well, there's Ukrainian kids being killed, and that's the responsibility of Vladimir Putin. An unprovoked war is his cause. But we had deterrence, Tim. For four years, Vladimir Putin didn't invade Europe. He invaded it in 2014. For four years he didn't and then as soon as we left he went after it again that is because he perceived the risk as being lower. I'm convinced of that whether it was the failure in Afghanistan where we got thirteen Americans killed whether it was President Biden's statement that says you know a minor incursion that might be okay. Those are the things that Vladimir Putin in his mind says, boy, my risk is low. And so we could have done more to deter. The fact that the Biden administration is providing systems to the Ukrainians to help them defend themselves, I applaud. But man, they've been slow. And man, they've been late. And man, they have feared Vladimir Putin being provoked. I have to tell you, I think he's done been provoked. And the cautiousness has allowed this war to continue to go on. We could have ended this thing more quickly. We could have provided these systems to them at the front end, and the Ukrainians would have done the heavy lifting. And I'm convinced we wouldn't be where we are today.",
